# Ways of travelling, from on foot to by plane, with how do you go and a reason why.
lesson language-getting-to-school v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "How I get to school"
  goal "Say how you get to school and how others travel, in [[language.name]], and ask someone how they go."

  look {
    scene 32x10 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row ways space=2 align=end {
          bus b windows=4 on=2 sign="12"
          ferry f cars=1 windows=4
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      To say how you travel, say [[transport.i-go-by-bus]], I go by bus, or
      [[transport.i-go-on-foot]], I go on foot. In the ferry town some children go to school
      [[transport.by-boat]], by boat. To ask, say [[phrase.how-do-you-go-to-school]], how do you go
      to school?
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.getting-to-school-name k=4
    }
    show language.getting-to-school-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.getting-to-school-meaning k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.getting-to-school-write k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.getting-to-school-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.getting-to-school-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.getting-to-school-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show language.getting-to-school-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.getting-to-school-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.getting-to-school-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.getting-to-school-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.getting-to-school-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.how-do-you-go-to-school]] How do you go to school? [[transport.i-go-on-foot]] I go on foot. [[transport.i-go-by-bus]] I go by bus."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the bus and the train taken for each other, and the boat and the
    plane: each naming question describes the journey and offers the way most often mixed up. The
    minutes on the puzzle's table are made up for the sums. The step up from the town and the
    directions of grades three and four is how one travels rather than where to, with a reason for
    the choice. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed
    without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read
    aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, act out each way of travelling and name
    it. Off the page, count the ways people travel past your window for five minutes and name each
    in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory
    from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions; express opinions and respond to
    those of others; seek clarification and help"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language
    activities: "Activities to ask and answer simple questions about each other's likes and wants,
    etc."; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic
    phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[transport.by-bike]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[transport.on-foot]] and [[transport.by-bus]] and the words to write are [[transport.train]] and [[transport.car]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[transport.by-boat]] and [[transport.by-plane]] and the words to write are [[transport.bus]], [[transport.bike]] and [[transport.car]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
